vation namespace aren\'t being scraped by Prometheus. Or perhaps the Prometheus configuration isn\'t set up to scrape those services. \n\nAlternatively, the services might not be instrumented with the correct metrics. For example, if they\'re using a different metric name, like "http_requests" instead of "http_requests_total", or if they\'re not using the same labels. \n\nSince the user has already checked the deployments and services, maybe the next step is to check the actual pods to see if they\'re exposing metrics. They could use kubectl to check the logs of the Prometheus server to see if there are any errors in scraping the targets. Or check the Prometheus configuration to see if the targets are correctly set up. \n\nBut the available tools here are limited. The user can use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to check the Prometheus configuration or the logs. Alternatively, they could check the services\' endpoints to see if they\'re accessible. \n\nWait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. They could use that to check the Prometheus configuration. For example, run "kubectl get configmap -n monitoring prometheus-config" to see the scrape configurations. Or check the Prometheus service\'s logs with "kubectl logs <prometheus-pod> -n monitoring". \n\nAlternatively, they could check the services in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if they have the correct annotations for metrics. But since the user is in the thinking stage, they need to choose the next tool. \n\nGiven that the metrics are not showing up, the next logical step is to check the Prometheus configuration to see if the targets are correctly set up. So using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the Prometheus config or logs. \n\nAlternatively, check the services in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if they have the correct labels. But the user might not have access to that. \n\nSo the best tool here would be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to check the Prometheus configuration or logs. Let\'s se