I need to first verify where Prometheus is deployed. By listing all namespaces, I can identify the correct namespace containing Prometheus resources (e.g., ServiceMonitors, PrometheusConfigs). This will help locate the correct config map or service account for Prometheus scraping settings.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get namespaces"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 407, 'prompt_tokens': 3371, 'total_tokens': 3778, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfde9-de5e-7161-ac33-18fb4ce93f5b-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3371, 'output_tokens': 407, 'total_tokens': 3778}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='53d3cec1-72c6-4adf-a993-093ad43d8b99'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their hotel reservation application, which consists of many microservices. They\'ve already checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running normally. But when they tried to get metrics using Prometheus, there were no results. Then they tried to check the Prometheus config in the "monitoring" namespace, but that namespace wasn\'t found.\n\nSo, the next step is to figure out where Prometheus is actually deployed. The user tried to get the namespaces, which should list all existing namespaces in the cluster. If the "monitoring" namespace doesn\'t exist, maybe Prometheus is in a different namespace, or perhaps it\'s not set up correctly. \n\nBy running "kubectl get namespaces", the user can see all the namespaces available. This will help identify the correct namespace where Prometheus is running. Once they know the right namespace, they can check the Prometheus configuration files, like the ServiceMonitor or PrometheusConfig, to see if the job name or targets are misconfigured. \n\nIf the output shows that the "monitoring" namespace isn\'t there, maybe Prometheus is deployed in another namespace like "default" or something else. Alternatively, maybe the Prometheus instance isn\'t set up at all, which would explain why the metrics aren\'t being scraped. \n\nAfter identifying the correct namespace, the next step would be to check the Prometheus configuration to ensure that the job name "hotel-reservation" is correctly specified and that the targets (like the services exposing metrics) are properly defined. If the job name is different, that could be why the metrics query isn\'t returning any data. \n\nAlso, checking the logs of the Prometheus pod might help identify if there are any errors in scraping the targets. But first, confirming the namespace is crucial. So the next tool call should be to get all namespaces to find where Prometheus is located.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get namespaces"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8933d0415ec596af', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 408, 'prompt_tokens': 4380, 'total_tokens': 4788, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfde9-f15f-7e83-bcba-bbd0fc06873c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get namespaces'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8933d0415ec596af', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4380, 'output_tokens': 