user can see all the namespaces available. This will help identify the correct namespace where Prometheus is running. Once they know the right namespace, they can check the Prometheus configuration files, like the ServiceMonitor or PrometheusConfig, to see if the job name or targets are misconfigured. \n\nIf the output shows that the "monitoring" namespace isn\'t there, maybe Prometheus is deployed in another namespace like "default" or something else. Alternatively, maybe the Prometheus instance isn\'t set up at all, which would explain why the metrics aren\'t being scraped. \n\nAfter identifying the correct namespace, the next step would be to check the Prometheus configuration to ensure that the job name "hotel-reservation" is correctly specified and that the targets (like the services exposing metrics) are properly defined. If the job name is different, that could be why the metrics query isn\'t returning any data. \n\nAlso, checking the logs of the Prometheus pod might help identify if there are any errors in scraping the targets. But first, confirming the namespace is crucial. So the next tool call should be to get all namespaces to find where Prometheus is located.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get namespaces"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8933d0415ec596af', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 408, 'prompt_tokens': 4380, 'total_tokens': 4788, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfde9-f15f-7e83-bcba-bbd0fc06873c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get namespaces'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8933d0415ec596af', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_ca