rvices, or the Prometheus server isn\'t configured to scrape them. They could check the services in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if they have endpoints exposing metrics, like /metrics endpoints. If the services don\'t expose metrics, that would explain why Prometheus isn\'t scraping them.\n\nAlternatively, the user could check the logs of the services in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if there are any errors related to metrics exposure. For example, if a service is not exposing /metrics, Prometheus won\'t scrape it. They could use kubectl logs to check the logs of the pods in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if there are any issues with the metrics server or the service itself.\n\nAnother possibility is that the Prometheus server is not properly configured to scrape the services. The user might need to check the Prometheus configuration file, which could be in a ConfigMap in the "observe" namespace. If they can\'t access the ConfigMap, maybe they can check the Prometheus server\'s own logs or configuration files to see the scraping targets.\n\nIn summary, the user needs to verify if the metrics are being exposed by the services, if the Prometheus server is correctly configured to scrape them, and if there are any access issues preventing the configuration from being retrieved. Since the current approach is blocked by access permissions, they might need to look for alternative ways to inspect the Prometheus configuration or check the services\' metrics endpoints directly.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The error indicates restricted access to the "observe" namespace. To avoid permission issues, I will directly check the Prometheus server\'s configuration in the "observe" namespace by inspecting its config map or secrets. This will reveal scraping targets and job names without listing all resources.  \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n observe prometheus-k8s -o yaml"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 920, 'prompt_tokens': 4393, 'total_tokens': 5313, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdea-a496-7290-ad62-5557cd50f601-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4393, 'output_tokens': 920, 'total_tokens': 5313}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bc796304-8f96-4b18-b31e-89ad8461bf0f'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. They\'ve been using various tools to check the services, deployments, metrics, and configurations. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments, services, and tried to get metrics using PromQL but didn\'t find any data. They also tried to look into Prometheus configurations but ran into namespace and permission issues.\n\nFirst, the user started by listing deployments in the hotel-reservation namespace, which gave a list of services like frontend, geo, user, etc. Then they checked Grafana services and found jaeger-all-in-one, geo, user. Next, they tried to get metrics for specific operations but got empty results. That suggested that either the metrics aren\'t being collected or the queries were incorrect.\n\nThey then tried to check the Prometheus configuration by looking into config maps in the monitoring namespace, but that namespace didn\'t exist. Then they listed all namespaces and found the "observe" namespace. They tried to get ServiceMonitors and config maps there but faced errors, possibly due to incorrect resource names or permissions.\n\nNow, the user is trying to get the config map for prometheus-k8s in the observe namespace. If that command fails, maybe the correct config map has a different name. Alternatively, they might need to check the Prometheus server\'s own configuration or look into secrets where the scraping targets are defined. Another approach could be to check the Prometheus server\'s logs or configuration files directly if access is allowed. Since they\'re facing permission issues, perhaps they need to use a different service account or check the Prometheus server\'s service configuration to see what targets it\'s scraping. If the job name is incorrect, that would explain why the metrics aren\'t showing up. Alternatively, the services might not be exposing metrics properly, or the Prometheus server isn\'t configured to scrape them. The next step would be to verify the scraping targets in Prometheus\'s configuration and ensure that the job name matches what\'s being used in the metrics queries.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get secret -n observe prometheus-k8s-alertmanager-main -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93134abf1d94b8b1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 460, 'prompt_tokens': 5403, 'total_tokens': 5863, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdea-cfc2-70a0-b740-bc26ddbd1bdf-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get secret -n observe prometheus-k8s-alertmanager-main -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93134abf1d94b8b1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5403, 'output_tokens': 460, 'total_tokens': 5863}